# sys_clock.tkr — the §16 C2 fixture for `teko::sys`, the curated per-`#os` libc constants module.
# `src/sys/sys.tks` (this fixture's local mirror at `src/sys/sys.tks`) declares `CLOCK_REALTIME`
# and `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` TWICE, once per `#os("linux")`/`#os("macos")` block — the build-time §17
# prune keeps EXACTLY the block matching the compiled target, before the checker ever sees the
# other one, so there is no duplicate-symbol clash. On linux the surviving values are
# `CLOCK_MONOTONIC == 1` and `CLOCK_REALTIME == 0` (glibc `bits/time.h`); on macos they would be
# `CLOCK_MONOTONIC == 6` and `CLOCK_REALTIME == 0` (Darwin `<time.h>`). The exit code
# `CLOCK_MONOTONIC * 10 + CLOCK_REALTIME` names which block survived: 10 on linux, 60 on macos —
# proving the prune resolved the LINUX definitions on this host.

Feature: sys_clock — the teko::sys #os("linux")/#os("macos") CLOCK_* constants survive the §17 prune

  Scenario: linux_clock_consts_survive — on linux CLOCK_MONOTONIC*10+CLOCK_REALTIME exits 10
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 10
